WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.536
This is a simulated robot installation:
one robot arm at a kitchen table.

2
00:00:05.536 --> 00:00:10.335
Its policy, the model that turns camera
images into motion, is fixed.

3
00:00:10.335 --> 00:00:14.186
Agents watch the installation, run
experiments on it,

4
00:00:14.186 --> 00:00:17.800
and change how the installation uses that
policy.

5
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.800
First story: make this one job.

6
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.745
The robot already has skills.

7
00:00:25.745 --> 00:00:31.874
Each skill is one task on this table, done
by the policy under its own instruction.

8
00:00:31.874 --> 00:00:35.165
Here, each one runs alone and delivers.

9
00:00:35.165 --> 00:00:39.100
The policy does not change anywhere in
this film.

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:47.146
A shift lead asks for one job: two skills
in a row, ending in a stated arrangement.

11
00:00:47.146 --> 00:00:50.625
The lead adds a rule about the order.

12
00:00:50.625 --> 00:00:53.394
The agent proposes the bar:

13
00:00:53.394 --> 00:00:59.572
the job must deliver on at least 18 of 20
fresh starts it was never run on.

14
00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:04.400
The lead approves that bar before any
program is tested.

15
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.752
The obvious chain switches to the next
skill the moment the first one reports

16
00:01:10.752 --> 00:01:11.888
success.

17
00:01:11.888 --> 00:01:14.953
That is the signal published scores count.

18
00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:20.403
But it can fire while the hand still holds
the item, and the arrangement ends wrong.

19
00:01:20.403 --> 00:01:23.071
On the {release_n} reserved starts,

20
00:01:23.071 --> 00:01:26.932
the obvious chain delivers on
{release_naive_delivered}.

21
00:01:26.932 --> 00:01:29.600
A success signal is not a delivery.

22
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.207
The agent reads the object traces and
writes its own check:

23
00:01:35.207 --> 00:01:40.605
did a later step undo an earlier one, and
is everything released and at rest?

24
00:01:40.605 --> 00:01:43.031
It finds the failing moment.

25
00:01:43.031 --> 00:01:46.974
Then it tests transitions on the
installation itself,

26
00:01:46.974 --> 00:01:49.400
2 development starts in all.

27
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.387
The agent freezes its program and runs it
on {release_n} reserved starts it has

28
00:01:55.387 --> 00:01:56.572
never seen.

29
00:01:56.572 --> 00:02:00.281
It delivers on {release_delivered}; the
bar was {release_k}.

30
00:02:00.281 --> 00:02:04.865
The obvious chain, on the same starts,
delivers on {release_naive_delivered}.

31
00:02:04.865 --> 00:02:06.874
Rescued: {release_rescued}.

32
00:02:06.874 --> 00:02:08.779
Broken: {release_broken}.

33
00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:12.900
Then the agent explains the change to the
shift lead in plain words.

34
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.959
The job and the check join the
installation's job list,

35
00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:22.280
and an operator runs the job with one
command from a fresh start.

36
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.636
Then a second request arrives.

37
00:02:24.636 --> 00:02:28.283
The agent runs the saved check and reuses
its method.

38
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:30.303
Job one took 1 sessions,

39
00:02:30.303 --> 00:02:34.287
2 simulated starts and 1 minutes of the
agent's model time.

40
00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:37.260
Job two took {job_two_sessions} sessions,

41
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:41.300
{job_two_starts} starts and
{job_two_model_minutes} minutes.

42
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.129
The robot policy stayed the same.

43
00:02:45.129 --> 00:02:50.076
The installation gained {library_jobs}
jobs and a check it did not have this

44
00:02:50.076 --> 00:02:51.200
morning.

45
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.139
Every clip in this film is a recorded run.

46
00:02:56.139 --> 00:02:58.700
The record lists each one.
